  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 314 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1:  Although much less widespread than the last few days, \nshower activity continues across the region this afternoon as \nsurface low pressure continues to shift eastward. Precipitation \namounts have been fairly light this afternoon, generally 0.5 inches \nor so across Rutland and Windsor counties where showers have been \nmore persistent. A few rumbles of thunder cannot be entirely ruled \nout this afternoon, especially with increased breaks in the cloud \ncover and some modest surface-based CAPE values near 500 J/kg or so \nper SPC mesoscale analysis.  \n\nHigh pressure will build into the region for the end of the week and \nthe start of the weekend, bring some drier conditions for the next \nfew days. With abundant surface moisture after the recent rainfall \nand the building high, patchy dense fog development looks favorable \novernight tonight and possibly again tomorrow night into Saturday.\nmorning. A few stray showers may be possible tomorrow, especially \nnear the high terrain but all in all it should be a much drier day. \nHigh temperatures for tomorrow and Saturday look to be fairly \nseasonable, with highs in the upper 70s to mid 80s. Sunday looks to \nstart dry, with increasing chances for precipitation as the day \nprogresses.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Another wet period remains on track for early next week, \nalthough there is a shift towards greater likelihood of most \nrainfall occurring primarily Monday-Monday night, particularly in \nVermont. If model trends continue, PoPs on Sunday will continue to \ndecrease and PoPs on Monday increase further.\n\nA complex upper air pattern with another slow moving system, this \ntime expected to track farther south across the Midwest, will lead \nto a surge of moisture and precipitation over the eastern seaboard \nas jet streaks oriented from south to north develop out ahead of the \nsystem. This setup will support a plume of deep, tropical moisture \nfrom the Gulf and Atlantic, described well by the Weather Prediction \nCenter in their Excessive Rainfall Outlook this morning in the days \n4-5 period. As a result, we would see some training heavy rain \nshowers (PWAT axis near 2 inches with warm cloud depths greater than \n12,000 feet), even amidst minimal instability. \n\nHowever, many ensemble members are opening up this upper level low \nover the weekend, and a deepening low pressure system moves \nnortheastward dragging a cold front or series of boundaries eastward \nthrough the region. The operational ECMWF continues to show this \ntype of event. A lack of a stationary boundary would limit the risk \nof excessive rainfall, although in this scenario would could see \nimpactful wind gusts, especially across northern New York, and \nintroduce a threat of severe thunderstorms more than flooding. Given \nlarge spread in model guidance at this time, it is a bit of a wait \nand see process today. With the wetter soil conditions ahead of this \nsystem across much of southern and western Vermont and northern New \nYork, there is some concern for greater localized flood risk during \nthis upcoming event if ingredients come together in this same area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: The middle to later part of next week is fairly \nuncertain as it is somewhat dependent on how the system early next \nweek plays out. Generally temperatures will tend to be on the warmer \nside of normal, dependent on cloud cover. Upper level ridging is \ncurrently favored on Tuesday and Wednesday, reducing the chances of \nshowers and thunderstorms and widespread cloud cover, relative to \nThursday. All together, the most likely warm and dry day appears to \nbe on Wednesday. However, the pattern looks unsettled enough that \nthere may be some showers each day in at least portions of our \nregion. The next, best chance of thunderstorms (roughly 30-40% per \nGEFS machine learning guidance) appears to be next Thursday along \nwith a modest threat (10-15%) of severe weather.",
